Let $r\geq 2$ be an integer. If a saddle $P$ of $f\in {Diff}^r(M)$ is in the support $\Lambda$ of a Eulerian heteroclinic graph that has mechanically no dominated splitting of indices $i\in I$, then for any open set $U$ containing $\Lambda$, there exists an open set ${\mathcal U}\subset {Diff}^r(M)$ such that:
\begin{itemize}
\item $f$ is in the closure of ${\mathcal U}$,
\item for any $g$ in ${\mathcal U}$, the continuation $P_g$ of the saddle $P$ is in an infinitely connected heteroclinic graph $\Delta_g$ supported in $U$, that contains infinitely many mechanical obstructions to domination of each index $i\in I$.
\end{itemize}

Let $r\geq 2$. Let $\Lambda$ be a homoclinic tangency for a diffeomorphism $f\in {Diff}^r(M)$.
Then ${\mathcal G}(\Lambda)$ is precisely the set of possible limit Lyapunov maps: $\tau\in {\mathcal G}(\Lambda)$ if and only if there are
\begin{itemize}
\item a sequence of diffeomorphisms $f_n$ converging to $f$ in ${Diff}^r(M)$
\item a sequence of periodic measures $\mu_n\in {\mathcal M}(f_n)$ where the supports $\mathrm{supp} \mu_n$ converge to a subset of $\Lambda$ for the Hausdorff topology,
\end{itemize}
such that $\tau=\lim_{n\to \infty}\sigma(\mu_n)$. Moreover, for any open set  $U\supset \Lambda$, there exists an open set ${\mathcal U}\subset {Diff}^r(M)$

such that:
\begin{itemize}
\item $f$ is in the closure $\overline{{\mathcal U}}$,
\item for any diffeomorphism $g$ in a residual subset ${\mathcal R}\subset {\mathcal U}$, if $\Pi_{g_{|U}}$ is the set of Lyapunov maps of periodic measures for the restriction $g_{|U}$, then the closure $\overline{\Pi_{g_{|U}}}$ is a neighborhood  in ${\mathbb R}^d$ of the polytope ${\mathcal G}(\Lambda)$.
\end{itemize}

Let $r\geq 1$ be an integer and let $P$ be a saddle point for a diffeomorphism $f$. Let ${\mathcal U}\subset {Diff}^r(M)$ be an open neighborhood of $f$ such that 
\begin{itemize}
\item the continuation $P_g$ of $P$ is defined for $g\in {\mathcal U}$,
\item for all $1\leq i< d$, there is a dense subset ${\mathcal D}_i \subset {\mathcal U}$ of diffeomorphisms $g$ such that the chain-recurrent class ${\mathcal C}(P_g,g)$ has mechanically no domination of index $i$. 
\end{itemize}
Then there is a residual set ${\mathcal R}\subset {\mathcal U}$ of diffeomorphisms $g$ such that the homoclinic class $\operatorname{Hom}(P_g,g)$ is the Hausdorff limit of a sequence of sinks or sources.

For any integer $r\geq 1$, for any diffeomorphism $f$ in a residual subset ${\mathcal R} \subset {Diff}^r(M)$, 
if the chain-recurrent class ${\mathcal C}(P,f)$ of a saddle $P$ has mechanically no domination of any index, that is:

 if, for any index $1\leq i<d$, there is a cycle of basic sets that contains the saddle $P$ and either
\begin{itemize}
\item a periodic point $Q$ of period $p$ such that the eigenvalues $\lambda_1,...,\lambda_d$ of the derivative $Df^p$ at $Q$, counted with multiplicity and ordered by increasing moduli, satisfy $|\lambda_i|=|\lambda_{i+1}|$,
\item or a non-transverse intersection point of the stable and unstable laminations of to two basic sets $K,L$ of index $i$ (possibly $K=L$),
\item or, more generally a bundle tangency of index $i$, as defined later in~section~3.1,
\end{itemize}
then the homoclinic class $\operatorname{Hom}(P,f)$ is the Hausdorff limit of a sequence of sinks or sources.

Let $r\geq 2$ be an integer. Let $\Lambda$ be a homoclinic tangency or a heteroclinic cycle for a diffeomorphism $f\in {Diff}^r(M)$. One has a sharp dichotomy:
\begin{itemize}
\item either $\Lambda$ is volume-hyperbolic,
\item or  Newhouse phenomena occur $C^r$-close to $\Lambda$
\end{itemize}

Fix real numbers $C>0$ and $0<\alpha<1$. Let $\Lambda$ be a homoclinic tangency or a heteroclinic cycle for a diffeomorphism $f\in {Diff}^{1+\alpha}_C(M)$. One has a sharp dichotomy:
\begin{itemize}
\item either $\Lambda$ is volume-hyperbolic,
\item or there is an open set ${\mathcal U}\subset {Diff}^{1+\alpha}_C(M)$ such that 
 \begin{itemize}
 \item $f$ is in the closure of ${\mathcal U}$
 \item and any $g$ in a residual set ${\mathcal R}\subset {\mathcal U}$ has infinitely many sinks or sources.
\end{itemize}
\end{itemize}
